draft_notes.explanation:
In the canonical frame the floor fills the bottom two rows. The gaps are runs of background (colour 0) in the second-to-last row. Each floating box is erased and then stamped into the gap whose width equals its 'length'. The length is the box's extent along the floor if its perpendicular extent is 2; otherwise it is the perpendicular extent. The stamp fills the gap line and the line just beyond it, away from the floor.

The floor side depends on the D4 transform. A horizontal reflection leaves 'down' unchanged, and each clockwise rotation maps the bottom edge to left, then top, then right. So for k = d4 % 4 the factory picks the side from [bottom, left, top, right][k]. The emitted program works in visible coordinates for that side:
- the gap line is row H-2, row 1, column 1 or column W-2;
- runs are scanned along that line;
- the perpendicular and along extents are measured on the correct axes;
- the box is stamped into the two lines next to the floor (rows H-3..H-2, rows 1..2, columns 1..2 or columns W-3..W-2).

The background constant is palette[0]. Objects are single-colour 4-connected components of non-background cells outside the two floor lines. Each gap is used at most once, so ties are handled.
